WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.431
A buffer overflow occurs when code reads
or writes beyond the intended boundary of a

2
00:00:04.511 --> 00:00:04.904
buffer.

3
00:00:04.984 --> 00:00:07.174
In many low-level programming
environments,

4
00:00:07.254 --> 00:00:11.016
that class of mistake has historically
caused severe reliability and security

5
00:00:11.096 --> 00:00:11.602
failures.

6
00:00:11.682 --> 00:00:13.198
The defensive lesson is simple:

7
00:00:13.278 --> 00:00:17.209
memory boundaries matter because they
define which data an operation is allowed to

8
00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:17.626
reach.

9
00:00:17.706 --> 00:00:19.446
Rust changes the default risk model.

10
00:00:19.526 --> 00:00:22.896
In safe Rust, developers normally work
through checked abstractions,

11
00:00:22.976 --> 00:00:26.233
ownership rules, borrowing rules, and
type-aware access patterns.

12
00:00:26.313 --> 00:00:27.997
Arrays, vectors, slices, strings,

13
00:00:28.077 --> 00:00:31.053
and byte buffers carry information about
length and validity.

14
00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:35.064
Safe APIs are designed so out-of-bounds
memory access is not an ordinary outcome of

15
00:00:35.144 --> 00:00:36.380
a small indexing mistake.

16
00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:40.166
The point of this course is not to
memorize old failure modes or learn offensive

17
00:00:40.246 --> 00:00:40.863
techniques.

18
00:00:40.943 --> 00:00:44.425
The point is to understand why buffer
boundaries matter and how Rust helps

19
00:00:44.505 --> 00:00:48.323
developers build software where invalid
memory access is prevented by default.

20
00:00:48.403 --> 00:00:51.380
That matters for parsers, network
services, file processing,

21
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.762
embedded code, libraries, and backend
systems.

22
00:00:53.842 --> 00:00:57.267
Rust's protections are strongest when code
stays in safe abstractions.

23
00:00:57.347 --> 00:01:00.099
Risk can re-enter at unsafe blocks, raw
pointer handling,

24
00:01:00.179 --> 00:01:03.324
foreign function interfaces, platform
APIs, device interfaces,

25
00:01:03.404 --> 00:01:05.481
and custom low-level buffer abstractions.

26
00:01:05.561 --> 00:01:09.492
Those boundaries need explicit review
because the compiler may not be able to prove

27
00:01:09.572 --> 00:01:10.808
the full buffer contract.

28
00:01:10.888 --> 00:01:12.684
The defensive goal is layered design.

29
00:01:12.764 --> 00:01:13.607
Prefer safe Rust.

30
00:01:13.687 --> 00:01:15.708
Make buffer ownership and length visible.

31
00:01:15.788 --> 00:01:17.529
Validate input at trust boundaries.

32
00:01:17.609 --> 00:01:19.406
Control allocation and resource use.

33
00:01:19.486 --> 00:01:21.339
Keep unsafe code small and documented.

34
00:01:21.419 --> 00:01:23.160
Wrap external behavior in safe APIs.

35
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:27.115
Test boundary cases over time so buffer
safety remains part of normal engineering

36
00:01:27.195 --> 00:01:27.700
practice.
